{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: dap-family note at 8d865eb5 (tree of 64b3ec8a), notes/2026-11-23-dap-family.md, against main b0a6439a.\n\nI read the whole note and checked its dap facts in packages/contract/src/bounds.ts, definition.ts, scope.ts, packages/platform/src/membership.ts and docs/scopes.md at the head. The bounds of section 6 (16 item types, 64 acts, 12 values, 12 refs, 8 sends, 64 list elements), sendsPerEntry 104, fanOut 32, scopeEntries 100 000, the ten ScopeKinds, `redact: { slot }`, `copies` on `relate`, the `invite-member` fields (handle, role, inviteHash, inviteEnds) and the 55 ms per 1 000 entries figure all match. Four findings block; four are minor.\n\nBlocking\n\nB1. Security: a member who is not the invitee can take a bound record (sections 3.3, 6.1). `join` carries the invitation secret as a plain `text` field (membership.ts, `join.fields.secret`). After the invitee joins, the secret sits in the membership journal, and every member who reads that journal can sign `redeem-claim` on P with it before the invitee does. The `redeem-claim` guards check state, hash and end time, and nothing binds the signer to the invited member. Fix: bind the claim to the invited member. For example, `offer-claim` carries the invitation's handle into the claim's `claimant` slot, and `redeem-claim` guards `signer` against that slot. Then the secret is not needed in P at all.\n\nB2. Accuracy: an application definition cannot check a secret against a hash. `join` checks it through platform code (`grant: { code: \"by-invitation\", row: \"P13\" }`). The Guard and Operand forms in definition.ts have no hash operator, so \"P checks the secret against the hash, as `join` does\" is not expressible. The fix in B1 removes the need. Otherwise list it as a gap in section 13, because F1 needs bound invitations.\n\nB3. Architecture: the copies cannot carry what section 5.3 relies on. docs/scopes.md (Relationship updates): the receiver keeps a copy of the owner's item state and the update's detail, keyed by owner, item and name. Three consequences:\n- A person holds copies from its children (`parent-of`) and its couples (`partner-in`), and none from its parents. \"The confirmed subjects of its parents\" cannot be read locally.\n- A copy carries the parentage or couple item's state, not whether the related person has a confirmed subject. A detail field set at the relate goes stale when that subject later claims, releases or changes.\n- A copy's state is the owner's state. The receiver cannot set it to `proposed` or confirm it: `Subject` is `on`, `also.\u003cname\u003e` or `scope`, and `AlsoRule` selects local items only. `confirm-link` and `decline-link` must write a local item that names the copy.\nRewrite 5.3 and the `confirm-claim` rule of 3.2 to match, or move confirmation by relatives to a later stage with its own gap row.\n\nB4. Architecture and privacy: detachment is a property of a field type in the definition, not of a record (`{ type: \"text\"; detached?: true }`). \"For a living person, the names, free text and transcriptions are detached\" (6.1) cannot vary by `living`. Say which slots are detached for every person. Also cover the values that stay in the journal for a living person: `lat`, `lng`, the `place` scope reference, `placeText` (not detached in the 6.1 table), the `date` record, and the fields of the `create` message that `add-person` sends from the circle, which land in the circle's journal and in the outbound message, out of reach of `redact` on P. Section 8.4 and G10 need these.\n\nMinor\n\nM1. The merge does not fit the send bounds (6.5). \"The duplicate copies its assertions to the survivor by its own sends\": up to 512 events and 1024 citations, against 8 sends for each handler, one fan-out of at most 32, and 104 sends for each entry. `relate redirect to its neighbours` hits the same fan-out limit for a parent of 512 children. State the batching, or add a gap row.\n\nM2. The 513th child. A `relate` past `copies` is refused `type-full` and keeps no copy. Say what the child's `add-parentage` does on that refusal; \"read from the index\" covers the read, not the refused send.\n\nM3. Guards that need data the scope does not have. `set-visibility` refuses `public` for a \"presumed living\" record (6.1), but presumed living depends on dates estimated from relatives (8.1), and dates are int records, not `time` slots. The guard can read only `living`. Likewise, a rule expression that converts Hebrew, Islamic or Julian dates into `sort` days is unproven in the profile's language. Mark both as publisher or client checks, or as unverified.\n\nM4. Internal consistency. Section 12 says \"copies are kept for children only\", but couples relate `partner-in` and merges relate `redirect`. The handler `member-of` (6.1) appears nowhere else, and its name is membership's platform code (P26). `unmerge` duplicates \"an undo is a second merge scope\". Section 8.4 keeps `sort` to the year, which contradicts the rule that `sort` agrees with y, m and d.\n\nConclusions\n\nArchitecture: sound in its main line. One scope for each person, parentage owned by the child, no fan-in on the write path, and the index and projections without authority hold up against docs/scopes.md. B3 corrects what copies can carry.\n\nSecurity: not acceptable until B1 and B2 are fixed. The anonymous path is sound: no keyless reader reaches a scope (3.4, 9.3). Visibility needs B4 before it states what erasure removes.\n\nSimplification:\n- Drop `edit-*`: principle 5 says assertions, not overwrites, so retract and add again covers it and keeps the history plainer.\n- Drop `unmerge`, and keep the second merge scope.\n- Drop `member-of`.\n- With B1, `redeem-claim` needs no secret, and `offered` with a bound claimant replaces the hash slots.\n- `family-merge` can wait for F6 and leave F0 to F5.\n- Seven claim states could become five: fold `withdrawn` into `declined` with a reason, or `lapsed` into `withdrawn`.\n\nStyle is clear and active. The checks named in the request were not rerun; this review reads the text and the code.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:64b3ec8a53abb0cf72fac3cd65e27578c8f21104","binding":"assigned","head":"8d865eb5d9c3a6aef2f5418d802cf195182b0916","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eae44c06c48e9f7132f528c901a0ceb51d0ca564\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:aa85bdaf0f256e390ffd92b082bf16a71a0b123d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eae44c06c48e9f7132f528c901a0ceb51d0ca564","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}